671:
(a) Except as provided in this article, no person shall discharge or cause to be discharged any of the following described waters or wastes into any public sewer:
(1) Any liquid or vapor having a temperature higher than 150 degrees Fahrenheit;
(2) Any water or waste containing fats, wax, grease, or oils, whether or not emulsified, in excess of 100 parts per million by weight which may solidify or become viscous at temperatures between 40 and 150 degrees Fahrenheit;
(3) Any gasoline, benzene, naphtha, fuel oil, motor oil, mineral spirits, commercial solvent, or other flammable or explosive liquid, solid, or gas;
(4) Any water or wastes that contain more than ten parts per million by weight of the following gases: hydrogen sulphide, sulphur dioxide, or nitrous oxide;
(5) Any ashes, cinders, sand, mud, straw, shavings, metal, glass, rags, feathers, offal, plastics, wood, paunch, manure, hair and fleshing, entails, lime slurry, lime residues, beer or distillery slups, chemical residues, paint or ink residues, cannery waste, bulk solids, thread, yarn, tar, latex, unground garbage, whole blood, paper dishes, paper cups, paper or plastic milk containers, strong acid, metal pickling wastes, concentrated plating solutions, or any other solid or viscous substance capable of causing obstruction to the flow in sewers or other interference with the proper operation of the sewage works;
(6) Any stormwater, surface water, ground water, roof runoff, subsurface drainage, uncontaminated cooling water, or unpolluted industrial process waters;
(7) Any waters or wastes having a pH lower than 5.5 or higher than 10.0 at any time, or having any other corrosive property capable of causing damage or hazard to structures, equipment or personnel of the works;
(8) Any waters or wastes containing iron, chromium, copper, zinc, and similar objectionable or toxic substances; or wastes of such concentration that any such material received in the composite sewage from the industrial plant exceed the limits for such materials as follows:
Fixed upper limits for constituents (parts per million by weight):
a. Cadmium .....5.0
b. Chromium .....3.0
c. Copper .....3.0
d. Cyanide .....0.0
e. Nickel .....0.1
f. Silver .....5.0
g. Tin .....5.0
h. Zinc .....3.0
i. Phenol .....0.5
(9) Any waters containing suspended solids of such character and quantity that unusual provision, attention, or expense is required to handle such materials at the sewage treatment facilities;
(10) Any noxious or malodorous gas or substance capable of creating a public nuisance or hazard to life or preventing entry of workmen into sewers for their maintenance, inspection, and repair;
(11) Any waters containing quantities of radioactive wastes or isotopes in excess of applicable state or federal regulations or recommendations;
(12) Any concentrated dye wastes, spent tanning solutions, or other wastes which are highly colored, or wastes which are of unusual volume, concentration of solids, or composition that may create obstruction to the flow in sewers or other interference with the proper operation and treated effluent of the sewerage works;
(13) Any waters or wastes containing other taste or odor producing substances in such concentrations exceeding limits which may be established as necessary, after treatment of the composite sewage, to meet the requirements of the state, federal, or other agencies having jurisdiction over such discharge to the receiving waters;
(14) Any waters or wastes containing substances which are not amenable to treatment or reduction by the sewage treatment processes employed or are amenable to treatment only to such degree that the sewage treatment facilities effluent cannot meet the requirements of the state, federal, or other agencies having jurisdiction over discharge to the receiving waters;
(15) Waters or wastes having a five-day biochemical oxygen demand in excess of 400 parts per million by weight on a 24-hour composite basis;
(16) The admission into the public sewers of any waters or wastes having a suspended solids content in excess of 600 parts per million by weight on a 24-hour composite basis;
(17) Scavenger wastes shall not be permitted to be disposed of in the sewer system; scavenger wastes are considered to be any putrid or offensive matter and the contents of septic tanks and cesspools; and
(18) All other materials and substances, chemicals or chemical compounds, and/or industrial wastes shall not be permitted to be discharged into the public sewer systems except as theretofore provided.
(b) Should waters or wastes be discharged or proposed to be discharged to the public sewers, where such waters contain substances or possess the characteristics prohibited by this article, the city may:
(1) Terminate admission of prohibited waters or wastes into the public sewers whenever discovered to exist;
(2) Refuse proposed admission of prohibited waters or wastes into the public sewers;
(3) Require pretreatment to a permissible condition of any waters or wastes as a condition to acceptance into the public sewers;
(4) Require control over the quantities and rates of waters or wastes entering the public sewers; and/or
(5) Require payment to cover the added capital and operating costs not covered by existing taxes or sewer charges, to cover the added cost of handling and treating otherwise prohibited wastes.
(c) Where pretreatment or control over quantities of wastes is required, the design and installation of the plants and equipment shall be subject to the review and approval of the state water quality control board and the city.
(d) Where privately owned pretreatment facilities are provided for any waters or wastes, they shall be maintained continuously in satisfactory and efficient operation by the owner thereof at his expense.
(e) The owner of any operation producing industrial wastes which enter public sewers shall install a final one-half-inch mesh screen in their effluent waste system immediately prior to entrance into the public sewer. There shall be no bypass around the screen. Such screen shall be locked in place and sealed with a city seal.
(f) When required by the city, the owner of any property served by a building sewer carrying industrial wastes shall install a suitable control manhole on the building sewer on his property to facilitate observation and sampling the measurement of wastes. Such manhole, when required, shall be accessible and safely located and shall be constructed in accordance with plans approved by the city. The manhole shall be installed by the owner at his own expense and shall be maintained by him so as to be safe and accessible at all times. The manhole shall be located on the industrial waste service connection with all sanitary wastes excluded.
(g) All measurements, tests, and analyses of the characteristics of waters and wastes to which reference is made in this article shall be determined in accordance with generally accepted laboratory procedures approved by the state water quality control board, and shall be determined at the control manhole provided in subsection (f) of this section, or upon suitable samples taken at such control manhole. In the event that no special manhole has been required, the control manhole shall be considered to be the nearest downstream manhole in the public sewer to the point at which the building sewer is connected.
(h) Any approval by the city of a type, kind, or capacity of an installation shall not relieve a person of the responsibility of revamping, enlarging, or otherwise modifying such installation to accomplish the intended purpose. Nor shall any written or verbal agreement as to limits of constituents or volumes of waters or wastes be considered as final approval for continuing operation. These limits shall be subject to constant study and change as considered necessary to serve their intended purpose.
(i) In the event of an accidental spill or unavoidable loss to the drains of any deleterious material, the industry concerned shall promptly notify the city of the nature of the spill, the quantity, and the time of occurrence.
(Code 1982, § 11-105)

